+{"id":"PERF-016","title":"P3 — resolve the git binary once instead of PATH-probing it on every spawn: 2612 execve attempts per lane-target run, 2294 of them ENOENT PATH probes (-13% unit / -11% gate at load 11-21; effect NOT resolvable at load 6-10)","status":"pending","priority":"P3","complexity":3,"reasoning":"Drafted by perf fire 2026-11-03 18-55-30, re-validated by 2026-11-03 19-51-49, 2026-11-04 01-21-15, 2026-11-04 03-41-13 and 2026-11-04 08-12-52 (series: ~/.hermes/stand-in/perf/code-factory/perf-2026-11-04-evidence.md). Attributed with a PATH shim and strace: the 77 `git` invocations per lane-target run are launched as bare `git` in argv, so CPython's execvp probes every PATH entry before the real binary — 2294 of 2401-2612 execve attempts in the run are ENOENT probes. Change: module-level `shutil.which(\"git\")` cache in `scripts/lane_target.py::_git` and `tests/test_lane_target.py::_git`, two files, no output/assertion/exit-code change. Measured effect by load regime: load 11-21 -> unit 1.740->1.513 s and gate 8.423->7.460 s CPU (-13% / -11%, resolvable); load 38-47 -> -8.7% / -2.8% (overlapping); load 7.4-8.4 -> -6.9% / -2.3% (unit not resolvable); load 6.3-10.4 -> -3.6% / +0.4% (not resolvable). So the CPU claim is regime-dependent and the deterministic claim is the execve/ENOENT count. This row was re-filed with corrected criteria by tick 2026-11-04-09-53-51, whose first filing wrongly proposed the memoised two_roots() candidate that the 08-12-52 fire had already refuted (spawns -21%, CPU unchanged) — the id was right, the candidate was wrong.","acceptance_criteria":"1. Paired CPU measurement of `tests/test_lane_target.py` (base vs patch, three runs each, alternated back-to-back) inside ONE quoted load regime, and the same for the whole gate (`bash tests/selftest.sh`). Require non-overlapping ranges before claiming a CPU win. State the regime in the row note and the audit doc. If the regime yields overlapping ranges (the fourth regime on record did: unit -3.6%, gate +0.4% at load 6.3-10.4), report the measured floor and DO NOT claim a CPU win — the resolvable result is then AC-4 only.\n2. `tests/test_lane_target.py` keeps 40 tests, the same test names, and prints `OK` (exit 0).\n3. Whole gate at the closing tip: `SELFTEST: PASS`, rc=0, 190 PASS rows at e0e73bd (re-count, do not copy), byte-identical PASS row text, no new SKIP row.\n4. Determinism arm (the invariant that survives any load regime): `strace -f -e trace=execve python3 -B tests/test_lane_target.py` shows <=500 execve attempts from a baseline >=2500 at e0e73bd, and <=200 of those are ENOENT PATH probes from a baseline of ~2294. Quote both counts in the audit doc.\n5. Numbers, commands, load regime, both directions of the claim (what was measured vs what was projected) and the honest remaining floor go in `docs/audits/code-factory-perf-2026-11-04-PERF-016.md`.\n6. Commit message carries AGENTS.md rule 2 and the `# ch:trace row=PERF-016` marker.","probe":"d=$(mktemp -d); strace -f -e trace=execve -o \"$d/x.log\" python3 -B tests/test_lane_target.py >/dev/null 2>&1; echo \"execve=$(grep -c 'execve(' \"$d/x.log\") enoent=$(grep -c ENOENT \"$d/x.log\")\"","created_at":"2026-11-04T10:01:33Z","updated_at":"2026-11-04T10:17:46Z","detail":{"fingerprint":"735da7a1d56003f87e1079b2cc283c9498f7db4633b20849e9d3c019c40c4c42","restored_by":"code-factory-perf-2026-11-04-09-53-51 (resumed pass)","restored_note":"Original filing (v1, tick 2026-11-04-01-21-15 series) and its v2 correction were written uncommitted; a sibling lane's board write replaced tasks.jsonl with its own copy and the row was lost. Restored byte-preservingly from the authoring pass's snapshot + its staged v2 texts, with the probe field filled from a command this restore executed (prints execve=2401 enoent=2294 at b626238).","measured_at":"b626238fdcce41f577c0a71ca509c4e10308eee0"}}
 {"id":"CF-087","title":"P3 — node-create's staged-set validator reads one spelling in two of its four staged files: a ${HERE}/ reference in the entry point and any reference added to the shipped lib.sh print a green --staged-files manifest, so the manifest certifies a node that dies on its first line","status":"pending","priority":"P3","complexity":3,"created_at":"2026-11-03T16:31:30Z","updated_at":"2026-11-03T16:31:30Z","detail":{"fingerprint":"qa-lane-readme-2026-11-03-cf087"},"reasoning":"Measured at 9131656 with the row's probe, run from the repo root. Control arm (literal `$HERE/unstaged.py` appended to scripts/project-node.sh): rc=1, `FAIL staged_set — project-node.sh references unstaged unstaged.py` — the matcher fires. Braced arm (`${HERE}/unstaged.py` in the same file): rc=0 and the four-line manifest prints, no failure. lib.sh arm (literal `$HERE/unstaged.py` appended to scripts/lib.sh, which ships inside the staged set and is sourced by both the entry point and the adapter): rc=0, four-line manifest, no failure. Cause is visible in scripts/node-create.sh: line 20 keeps a hand-written array `staged=(project-node.sh lib.sh disk_floor.py harness/install-$HARNESS.sh)` and the matcher at line 27 walks only `project-node.sh` and `harness/install-$HARNESS.sh` with pattern `\\$HERE/([a-zA-Z0-9_./$-]+)`, so two of the four staged members are never scanned and the brace form never matches. CF-075 is complete and its AC-1 requires a DERIVED set plus 'a check on the staged set exits nonzero when any referenced file is missing'; that clause does not hold at the closed tip. Filed as a distinct failure mode (validator coverage), not as a duplicate of CF-075 (a member was missing): the 2026-11-03 PERF-014 closure changed tests/test_lane_target.py and the node path, not this validator.","acceptance_criteria":"0. Reproduce on the current tree from the repo root: the `probe` field prints `braced-entry rc=0`, `literal-lib rc=0` and a four-line manifest, and exits 0 today.\n1. `node-create.sh --staged-files` exits nonzero and names the offending file when a runtime reference is added to ANY staged member (at minimum scripts/lib.sh), and when that reference is written as `${HERE}/x`, `$HERE/x` or `'$HERE/x'`; tests/selftest.sh carries one arm per spelling and one arm per staged file, and each arm asserts the NAMED `FAIL staged_set` row (not the substring FAIL, not a traceback).\n2. The staged set is derived rather than hand-listed: the set `--staged-files` prints is computed from the references the shipped scripts actually make, and a member the entry point needs but the set omits cannot pass; either the literal array is gone or it is asserted equal to the derived set.\n3. `bash tests/selftest.sh` prints SELFTEST: PASS at the fixed tip, and reverting the matcher to the two-file, one-spelling form makes the new fixture fail with a named row.","probe":"d=$(mktemp -d); mkdir -p \"$d/a/harness\" \"$d/b/harness\"; for x in a b; do cp scripts/node-create.sh scripts/project-node.sh scripts/lib.sh scripts/disk_floor.py \"$d/$x/\"; cp scripts/harness/install-hermes.sh \"$d/$x/harness/\"; printf 'X=1\\n' > \"$d/$x/unstaged.py\"; done; printf '\\n. \"${HERE}/unstaged.py\"\\n' >> \"$d/a/project-node.sh\"; printf '\\n. \"$HERE/unstaged.py\"\\n' >> \"$d/b/lib.sh\"; bash \"$d/a/node-create.sh\" --staged-files >\"$d/a.out\" 2>&1; echo \"braced-entry rc=$?\"; bash \"$d/b/node-create.sh\" --staged-files >\"$d/b.out\" 2>&1; echo \"literal-lib rc=$?\"; tail -1 \"$d/a.out\"; tail -1 \"$d/b.out\""}
 {"id":"CF-088","title":"The selftest board arm accepts any valid JSON, so the gate greens a board that scripts/boardctl validate rejects","status":"complete","priority":"P3","complexity":2,"reasoning":"Measured at 400a5ab by code-factory-releng-qa-2026-11-03-15-39-07. tests/selftest.sh section 7 (\"the board is valid JSONL\") counts a line as a row when json.loads accepts it, and nothing else in the suite runs scripts/boardctl validate against the real board: section 8 exercises only --help and the missing-executable path, and grep -n validate tests/selftest.sh returns one hit that labels a lane-target test. The guard's test_command is bash tests/selftest.sh (no workflows ship), so the validator is never executed by any automated check. The row probe appends six fixtures one at a time to a clone of the tracked board and reads both verdicts: null, [], \"just-a-string\", a duplicate .id and status \"bogus_status\" each print 'PASS 69/69 rows parse / SELFTEST: PASS' while boardctl validate prints '[error] row is not a JSON object', 'duplicate task id', or 'status ... not in vocabulary' and exits 1; a row with no .status slips through both (gate PASS, validator RESULT: OK with a warning), yet such a row is invisible to the selection every foreman uses (.status==\"pending\"). The clean-board control prints 'PASS 68/68 rows parse / SELFTEST: PASS' and 'OK (21 warning(s))'. So the two checks that the repo's own Tier-2 rubric names in the same sentence (\"SELFTEST: PASS and scripts/boardctl validate prints RESULT: OK\") disagree on every non-row fixture, and the one wired into the gate is the weaker of the two. Distinct from CF-048 (a truncated final line is skipped before parsing) and CF-069 (the arm dies green when python3 emits nothing: there the arm never runs, here it runs and counts a non-row as a row).","acceptance_criteria":"1. The selftest board arm fails on a line that is valid JSON but not a JSON object, naming the 1-based line number as boardctl validate does; the row probe's null, array and string fixtures print a gate FAIL instead of 'PASS n/n rows parse'.\n2. A duplicate .id and an out-of-vocabulary .status also fail the gate, either in the arm or by the gate running scripts/boardctl validate against the real board and propagating its exit 1; proven by the probe's duplicate-id and unknown-status arms printing a gate FAIL.\n3. A row with no .status is named as an error (not a warning) by at least one automated check, because such a row is unreachable for select(.status==\"pending\") selection; the probe's missing-status arm must stop reading 'validator: RESULT: OK'.\n4. The negative arms do not depend on boardctl being installed: when the executable is absent the arm either performs the object/duplicate/vocabulary checks itself or fails with the reason named, never a silent pass.\n5. A numbered tests/selftest.sh section covers the clean board as a positive control: it prints '<n>/<n> rows parse' with n equal to wc -l of tasks.jsonl and SELFTEST: PASS at the fixed tip, and the closing evidence shows the same section FAILing on the pre-fix tests/selftest.sh without the fix.\n6. bash tests/selftest.sh prints SELFTEST: PASS, scripts/boardctl validate prints RESULT: OK, and the closing commit is pushed with git rev-list --count origin/main..HEAD = 0.","created_at":"2026-11-03T16:36:49Z","updated_at":"2026-11-03T19:45:43Z","detail":{"measured_at":"400a5ab","source":"releng-qa","tick":"code-factory-releng-qa-2026-11-03-15-39-07","evidence":[{"run_id":"d0f1236d","ts":"2026-11-03T19:45:43Z","tree_fp":"f55adcfc91a934c1955fa621f3f3eb6c2b203d34b6a55ec7f9e8a0e9388160a5","artifacts":["docs/audits/code-factory-CF-088-2026-11-03.md"]}]},"commit_hash":"64057d5851895bdf3c349f6b4f5924c81cf93d16","worker_summary":"Selftest board arm now rejects non-object rows, malformed JSON, missing/invalid id, duplicate ids, missing status and out-of-vocabulary status with 1-based line numbers in one parser process; new section 7b proves nine fixture classes fail and the clean board passes 85/85. GitReins tier1+tier2 PASS (verdict 627fe92b).","probe":"#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# probe: the selftest gate's board arm and the shipped validator disagree on every non-row fixture.\n# Run from the root of a code-factory checkout. Writes only inside mktemp; no network.\nset -u\nroot=$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel) || { echo \"not a git checkout\"; exit 2; }\nt=$(mktemp -d) || exit 2\ntrap 'rm -rf \"$t\"' EXIT\ngit clone -q \"$root\" \"$t/cf\" || { echo \"clone failed\"; exit 2; }\ncd \"$t/cf\" || exit 2\nB=.coding-hermes/board/tasks.jsonl\n\ngate()  { bash tests/selftest.sh 2>&1 | grep -E 'rows parse|^SELFTEST' | tr '\\n' ' '; }\nvalid() { if command -v boardctl >/dev/null 2>&1 || [ -x scripts/boardctl ]; then\n            bash scripts/boardctl validate 2>&1 | grep -E '^RESULT|^\\[error\\]' | tr '\\n' ' '\n          else echo \"validator arm SKIPPED (boardctl absent)\"; fi; }\n\narm() { # $1 label, $2 fixture text\n  git checkout -q -- \"$B\"\n  printf '%s\\n' \"$2\" >> \"$B\"\n  printf '%-26s gate: %-38s validator: %s\\n' \"$1\" \"$(gate)\" \"$(valid)\"\n  git checkout -q -- \"$B\"\n}\n\narm \"null-line\"        'null'\narm \"array-line\"       '[]'\narm \"string-line\"      '\"just-a-string\"'\narm \"duplicate-id\"     \"$(git show HEAD:$B | jq -c 'select(.id==\"CF-082\")' | head -1)\"\narm \"unknown-status\"   '{\"id\":\"CF-999\",\"title\":\"bad status\",\"priority\":\"P4\",\"status\":\"bogus_status\"}'\narm \"missing-status\"   '{\"id\":\"CF-900\",\"title\":\"no status\",\"priority\":\"P4\"}'\nprintf '%-26s gate: %-38s validator: %s\\n' \"clean-board(control)\" \"$(gate)\" \"$(valid)\"\ngit checkout -q -- \"$B\"\n","guard_result":"PASS","ci_result":"SKIP"}
 {"id":"CF-089","title":"P3 — the README lanes census reports a failed page as an empty page: a 502 mid-run prints a short count and exits 0","status":"complete","priority":"P3","complexity":2,"reasoning":"README.md 'The lanes' recipe (lines 155-169) advances off by 500 and appends whatever jq extracts from each page; the curl and jq exit codes are discarded and nothing compares the enumerated project rows against the total the first request reported. Reproduced at 0fbfe1f against docs/audits/cf089-census-standin.py (two matching rows, at index 0 and 600; the page at offset=500 answers 502): the recipe printed 1 row and exited 0, the only signal a jq stderr line. The realistic trigger is a scheduler restart or load-shed between two pages. CF-065's no-total control still holds (RC=1), so this is the uncovered half of that row.","acceptance_criteria":"0. Reproduce at the current tree: run docs/audits/cf089-census-standin.py, then BASE=http://127.0.0.1:18999 bash docs/audits/cf089-census-recipe.sh prints 1 row and exits 0 while the stand-in holds 2 matching rows. 1. Any page whose fetch fails (curl nonzero), whose status is not 2xx, or whose body carries no .projects array aborts the census with a named message and a nonzero exit; one tests/selftest.sh arm per shape. 2. The recipe asserts coverage: the number of project rows enumerated equals the total reported by the first request, or it fails naming both measured numbers, so a short census cannot print a plausible small count. 3. The arms are falsifiable: reverting the check makes each fixture fail with the named row, and bash tests/selftest.sh prints SELFTEST: PASS at the fixed tip.","created_at":"2026-11-03T17:00:00Z","updated_at":"2026-11-03T19:02:11Z","probe":"bash docs/audits/cf089-census-standin.py & sleep 1; BASE=http://127.0.0.1:18999 bash docs/audits/cf089-census-recipe.sh; echo rc=$?","commit_hash":"61db6f7d6fc6324c2af29aec8ff9033c5f5d01ae","guard_result":"PASS","ci_result":"SKIP","worker_summary":"README census block and its mirror now fail closed on any bad page and assert rows-received == total before printing; stand-in rc 0/1 row becomes rc 1/named 502; live scheduler still prints 19 rows exit 0; selftest PASS; judge PASS at 61db6f7","detail":{"evidence":[{"run_id":"2566eeb5","ts":"2026-11-03T19:02:11Z","tree_fp":"6be9c8613db4b1810d96b713a1ecf261d5e6831c07b1a1cf7b5e7376ccc7855c","artifacts":["docs/audits/cf089-census-failclosed.txt"]}]}}
